--- !u!114 &943552574
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 943552573}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: d3316448f68d5f05bc3bd94a1e7091a0, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::EinsteinTelescope.Laser.LaserLevel
  title: Beam walking
  briefing: 'Beam walking: the laser now reaches the interferometer via two steering mirrors. First send it through Iris A and Iris B, then align the end mirrors.'
  objectiveText: 'In a real detector the laser does not point straight at the beam splitter. It is guided by <b>steering mirrors</b>, and technicians use small round holes called <b>irises</b> to check that the beam follows the right line.


    <b>Your task</b>

    1.  Use Steering mirror 1 and 2 to send the beam through the centre of Iris A and Iris B (within 1 cm).

    2.  Then align End mirror X and End mirror Y on the output detector, like in level 1.

    Everything must be aligned at the same time for 2 seconds. The monitor always names the first iris the beam does not pass yet.


    <b>Stars</b>

    Same rules as level 1: within 2 minutes and at most 1 safety incident for 3 stars.'
  controlsTip: 'PREV / NEXT cycles through 4 mirrors: Steering 1, Steering 2, End X, End Y. Beam walking: Steering mirror 1 centres the beam on Iris A, Steering mirror 2 on Iris B; repeat.'
  physicsText: "<b>1. A beam line has a position and a direction</b>\nTo put a beam on a fixed line you must set both, so you need two mirrors. That is why there are two steering mirrors and two irises.\n\n<b>2. Each mirror moves the spot on both irises</b>\n            \u0394x \u2248 2 \xB7 \u03B8 \xB7 L          (L = distance from the mirror to the iris)\n\u2022  Steering mirror 1 to Iris A:  L \u2248 1.9 m, one COARSE step moves the spot about 3.3 cm.\n\u2022  Steering mirror 2 to Iris B:  L \u2248 1.45 m, one COARSE step moves it about 2.5 cm.\nFINE steps are ten times smaller.\n\n<b>3. Beam walking</b>\nCentring Iris B with mirror 2 shifts the beam a little at Iris A, so you alternate: mirror 1 for Iris A, mirror 2 for Iris B, and repeat. Each round the errors get smaller, like solving two equations step by step.\n\n<b>4. The light hits these mirrors at 45\xB0</b>\nA left / right tilt still turns the beam by 2\u03B8, but an up / down tilt turns it by only about 1.4\u03B8 (2\u03B8 \xB7 cos 45\xB0)."
  laser: {fileID: 125457514}
  mirrors:
  - {fileID: 2121369656}
  - {fileID: 1582035401}
  - {fileID: 1303869559}
  - {fileID: 616282402}
  checkpoints:
  - {fileID: 1079777503}
  - {fileID: 290473976}
  checkpointLabels:
  - Iris A
  - Iris B
  checkpointHints:
  - 'Beam walking: Steering mirror 1 moves the beam onto Iris A, Steering mirror 2 centres it on Iris B. Repeat until both are centred.'
  - Use Steering mirror 2 to centre the beam on Iris B. If Iris A drifts off, correct it with Steering mirror 1 and come back.
  detector: {fileID: 1491480689}
