--- !u!114 &1956890467
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1956890466}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: d3316448f68d5f05bc3bd94a1e7091a0, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::EinsteinTelescope.Laser.LaserLevel
  title: Folded arms
  briefing: 'Folded arms: each arm has an extra fold mirror, like GEO600. Walk the beam through Iris A and B, centre it on the arm irises with the fold mirrors, then align the end mirrors.'
  objectiveText: 'Now each arm is <b>folded</b> by an extra mirror, like the GEO600 detector in Germany, which folds its 600 m arms so that the light travels 1200 m.


    <b>Your task</b>

    1.  Walk the beam through Iris A and Iris B with the two steering mirrors.

    2.  Use Fold mirror X to centre the beam on Iris X, and Fold mirror Y for Iris Y.

    3.  Align End mirror X and End mirror Y on the output detector.

    All four irises and the detector must be aligned at the same time for 2 seconds.


    <b>Stars</b>

    Same rules, but with 6 mirrors: work calmly from the laser outwards.'
  controlsTip: 'PREV / NEXT cycles through 6 mirrors: Steering 1, Steering 2, Fold X, End X, Fold Y, End Y. Work in that order: a mirror early in the path moves everything after it.'
  physicsText: "<b>1. Errors add up</b>\nEvery mirror the beam meets turns it by 2\u03B8. The spot error on the detector is the sum of all the mirror errors before it, each multiplied by its own distance L:\n            \u0394x \u2248 2 \xB7 (\u03B81 \xB7 L1 + \u03B82 \xB7 L2 + \u03B83 \xB7 L3 + ...)\n\n<b>2. Longer paths make it harder</b>\nEnd mirror X is now about 5.6 m from the detector (2.6 m to the fold mirror, 1.8 m to the splitter, 1.2 m to the detector). One COARSE step moves the spot 2 \xD7 0.0087 \xD7 5.6 \u2248 9.8 cm, one FINE step about 1 cm: exactly the size of the goal.\n\n<b>3. Work from the laser outwards</b>\nA mirror early in the path moves the beam on every iris and mirror after it. Fix the steering mirrors first, then the fold mirrors, then the end mirrors. If you change an early mirror later, check the irises after it again.\n\n<b>4. Why fold an arm?</b>\nA longer light path makes the tiny length change caused by a gravitational wave easier to measure. The Einstein Telescope is planned with 10 km arms, deep underground."
  laser: {fileID: 1859213919}
  mirrors:
  - {fileID: 966596612}
  - {fileID: 1944535833}
  - {fileID: 431355539}
  - {fileID: 569298593}
  - {fileID: 756872579}
  - {fileID: 46993059}
  checkpoints:
  - {fileID: 1914261313}
  - {fileID: 1312311490}
  - {fileID: 550630893}
  - {fileID: 33434459}
  checkpointLabels:
  - Iris A
  - Iris B
  - Iris X
  - Iris Y
  checkpointHints:
  - 'Beam walking: Steering mirror 1 moves the beam onto Iris A, Steering mirror 2 centres it on Iris B. Repeat until both are centred.'
  - Use Steering mirror 2 to centre the beam on Iris B. If Iris A drifts off, correct it with Steering mirror 1 and come back.
  - 'Fold mirror X sends the beam down the X arm: centre it on Iris X.'
  - 'Fold mirror Y sends the beam down the Y arm: centre it on Iris Y.'
  detector: {fileID: 625163222}
